            <div xml:id="d509">

                <pb facs="0543" n="435" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg435"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.841 Surtax/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacMurray</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-07-09T14:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 9, 1927—2
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>280. Your 711, July 9, 4 p.m. If the American shipping companies desire to take
                    the course suggested in first paragraph of above <pb facs="0544" n="436"
                        xml:id="pg436"/> message, you are authorized to do so. If, on the other
                    hand, American companies prefer to pay the surtax under protest, do not think
                    this Government should order them not to do so. There is no objection, of
                    course, to protesting against tonnage dues collected in violation of treaty but
                    if United States insists that the shipping companies shall only pay taxes
                    provided by the treaty, we assume an obligation to protect them against any
                    excess taxes even to the extent of using military or naval forces which we
                    notified in No. 61, February 15, 4 P.M.<anchor n="89" xml:id="d509-f1"/> this
                    Government is not prepared to do.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
